# Import the original world through source-authored FBX instead of replacing it…

Import the original world through source-authored FBX instead of replacing it
with an Unreal Landscape.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Import the original world through source-authored FBX instead of replacing
  it with an Unreal Landscape.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

The conversion boundary is implemented and tested. `fbx-export-world` emits
non-empty world and interior package scenes through the first-principles binary
FBX writer, applies the single `ReflectX` world-root policy, and preserves
source
mesh order plus position, normal, UV, and triangle evidence in the structural
guide. Four focused `world_fbx` tests cover exterior and interior guide
geometry,
root-policy and UV preservation, and source mesh ordering; all four pass.

The task remains open because native Unreal ingestion is still planned Phase 6
work. Current technical authority defines a staged FBX import, native read-back,
and World Partition publication transaction, but the repository does not yet
have validated execution evidence for that transaction. Current evidence
therefore proves the source-authored FBX boundary without claiming the final
Unreal import is complete.

## September 4 native world-import smoke

A live Unreal Engine 5.8.1 MCP smoke now exercises the source-authored world
path with a real terrain package rather than a synthetic mesh. A minimal
runtime-authority closure containing `L1_TERRA` and `l1z1` publishes the same
world-native target-safe `level-01-zones-l1z1.fbx` contract: 146 geometries,
77 materials, 77 textures, two repeated-index source records retained only in
the topology sidecar, and zero source or collision discard counts.

`SharImportEditor.SharImportToolset.ImportStaticMesh` imports that FBX into a
temporary `/Game/Generated/SHAR/SessionWorldImport` package without the prior
repeated-index FBX SDK fatal. Native read-back reports one LOD, 30,984 vertices,
17,580 triangles, 77 material slots, and bounds from
`[-563.95782470703125, -298.75360107421875, -15.877922058105469]` to
`[-152.61752319335938, -72.01856231689453, 39.550754547119141]`. The imported
asset is dirty as required by the no-save staging contract. Rollback deletes
both the asset and temporary folder, and an independent existence check returns
false afterward.

That smoke exposed missing target surface frames rather than missing source
geometry. The world FBX path now keeps source topology evidence separate while
completing only the target-required polygon-corner normal, tangent, and binormal
frames for render groups without authored normals. Authored normals remain
unchanged, and target-only repeated-index and zero-area omissions remain counted
in the topology sidecar rather than becoming silent source edits.

A fresh live import of the regenerated `l1z1` artifact through
`ImportWorldStaticMesh` succeeds as one `StaticMesh`. In the log segment emitted
by that invocation, Unreal reports zero nearly-zero binormal warnings, zero
nearly-zero normal warnings, zero degenerate tangent-basis warnings, and zero
missing-smoothing-group warnings. The same segment contains zero warnings and
zero errors overall.

This closes the shading-provenance blocker for the representative native smoke.
The task remains open because the acceptance criterion requires the complete
original world to be imported and published through the native Unreal
transaction, not only the bounded `l1z1` proof.
